=== w3-federal
_note: The federal safety-net cuts, moved out of w1-opening 2026-08-15 so the material has a scene of its own. Grounded in ~/HawaiiAppleseed/snap-medicaid-timeline (repo dtomkatsu/Hawaii-Appleseed-website, medicaidData/snapData arrays) — federal HR1 SNAP/Medicaid cuts, all already in effect by this session's Jan 2027 start. Figures: SNAP-Ed eliminated 2025-10-01 (-$2.5M/yr); ABAWD work-req age 18-54 -> 18-64 2026-10-01 hits 16,570 adults 55-64 + 10,303 households w/ teens 14-18; Medicaid narrower immigrant eligibility + 6-month (was 12) recert already dropped an est. 1,200-2,400 people. Refresh these if the timeline's data updates. NOTE the player's own bills have not been heard yet (h-fin deadline is week 5) — nothing here may imply a hearing on them.
talks: coalition, office, rep-x=talk-rep
actions: bench, rally

--- start
The joint briefing runs in a hearing room too small for it, which is how you can tell the department expected fewer people. What Congress did to the safety net in HR1 is no longer a forecast — every piece of it is already law and most of it is already running.
SNAP-Ed is gone: over $2.5 million a year the state used to spend on nutrition classes and farmers'-market programs, zeroed out in October 2025. Work requirements now reach ages 55 to 64 — 16,570 adults in Hawaiʻi who were exempt a year ago — plus 10,300 households with teenagers. On Medicaid, narrower eligibility and twice-yearly recertification have already dropped an estimated 1,200 to 2,400 people from the rolls, not because they stopped qualifying but because they missed a form.
None of it appears as a federal line item anymore. It appears here, in a state budget that is already $800 million short.
POLICY BRAIN: Read the caseload numbers, not just the caseload story. 16,570 and 10,300 are Hawaiʻi's own — DHS ran them, not a national estimate.
BASE BUILDING: Every family that lost coverage over a missed form, not a change in income, just learned exactly how fragile 'eligible' really is. That is an organizing conversation, not a caseload stat.
EDUCATOR: Nobody in this room disputes the numbers. What they don't have is a sentence that connects a missed recertification form to a tax bill. That sentence is the entire job.

* "Then our bills are the answer to this. Say it that way, every time."
  {+4 support, -1 influence}
  You watch it land. By afternoon two other organizations have picked up the framing, and one of them is a group that has never testified on a tax bill in its life.

* [POLICY BRAIN 11 white] "Get me the recertification data. How many were actually still eligible?"
  mods: EDUCATOR/4, influence/30
  pass:
    {+3 support, +3 influence, flag recert-data}
    It takes three calls and a very patient analyst, but the answer comes back: the overwhelming majority of the people dropped were still eligible when they were dropped. That is not a caseload decline. That is a paperwork failure with a body count, and now you can say so with a citation.
  fail:
    {-1 influence}
    What comes back is a summary table with the eligibility field aggregated away — technically responsive, practically useless. You can say 'many were still eligible.' You cannot say how many, and in this building the difference between those two sentences is everything.

* Sit with it. Take the room's temperature before saying anything.
  {+2 influence}
  Three department staff, two providers and a health-center director, and not one of them says the word 'taxes.' They have been told not to. It is useful to know exactly who is not allowed to make your argument for you.
